dsv41 patch 0081 (22.10.2026): KV offload, per-request load cap and file-tier I/O threads off the decode cores.
In use from image v24 (VLLM_DSV41_OFFLOAD_LOAD_FRACTION=0.45, VLLM_DSV41_OFFLOAD_IO_CPUS=0-4,10-13).
Why (cap): vLLM's tiering manager pins a CPU-tier chunk only when the load is prepared, not when it is promoted from
the file tier. A prefix that needs more chunks than the CPU tier holds evicts its own promoted chunks, its lookup
returns "pending" forever and the request never runs (seen 22.10.: a 76K-token prefix against a 963-chunk CPU tier,
EngineCore at 100 % CPU for 10 minutes). With the cap every request loads at most what fits the given share of the
CPU tier (computed from the number of CPU chunks and the offloaded groups' tokens per chunk); the rest is computed.
On DSv4.1 the offloaded groups have 128 and 192 tokens per chunk (0.0130 chunks per token): a 256 MiB CPU tier
(1,927 chunks with 0077) holds ~148K tokens, the cap at 0.45 is 66,432 tokens per request, so two large reloads fit
at once.
Why (I/O CPUs): the file tier's read/write threads live in the scheduler process and inherit its affinity (the X925
cores, with the launch threads); pread/pwrite and the CRC of 0056 then compete with decode.
Switches: VLLM_DSV41_OFFLOAD_LOAD_FRACTION (0 = off), VLLM_DSV41_OFFLOAD_IO_CPUS (empty = off).
